/"}]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"text-slate-700 font-medium truncate max-w-[200px]","children":"Cyber Insurance Explained for Small Businesses — Is It Worth It?"}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"relative text-white min-h-[45vh] flex items-end","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"absolute inset-0 bg-cover bg-center","style":{"backgroundImage":"url(https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1550751827-4bd374c3f58b?w=1600&q=80)"}}],["$","div",null,{"className":"absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-slate-950/90 via-slate-900/60 to-slate-900/10"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"relative z-10 max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-12 w-full","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 mb-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full font-medium bg-red-100 text-red-700","children":"Business"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs text-slate-300","children":"8 min read"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs text-slate-400","children":"·"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs text-slate-400","children":"5 March 2026"}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold mb-3 max-w-3xl drop-shadow-md","children":"Cyber Insurance Explained for Small Businesses — Is It Worth It?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-slate-200 max-w-2xl text-sm","children":"Ransomware attacks, data breaches, and business email compromise are devastating SMEs. Here's what cyber insurance covers and how to decide if you need it."}]]}]]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-12 bg-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"grid lg:grid-cols-3 gap-10","children":[["$","article",null,{"className":"lg:col-span-2","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-3 text-xs text-slate-500 border-l-2 border-red-300 pl-4 mb-8","children":["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-medium text-slate-700","children":"Written by "}],["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/about/","className":"text-red-600 hover:underline font-medium","children":"FraudProtection.co.nz Editorial Team"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"mx-2","children":"·"}],["$","span",null,{"children":["Updated ","March 2026"]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"prose prose-sm max-w-none","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"If someone told you there was a 1-in-5 chance your business would suffer a significant financial loss this year from a single type of risk, you'd insure against it immediately. Yet most small businesses still don't have cyber insurance — despite cyber attacks now affecting more businesses than any other crime type."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"Let's cut through the jargon and give you a clear picture of what cyber insurance is, what it covers, and whether your business needs it."}],["$","h2","2",{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-3","children":"What cyber insurance actually covers"}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"Cyber insurance is designed to help businesses survive a cyber incident. It's not primarily about stopping attacks — it's about paying for the response and recovery. Key covers typically include:"}],["$","p","4",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"*First-party costs (your own losses):*\n- Forensic investigation to determine what happened and how\n- System restoration and data recovery\n- Business interruption losses while systems are down\n- Ransomware extortion payment and negotiation (where legally permitted)\n- Customer and staff notification costs after a data breach\n- PR and crisis communications management"}],["$","p","5",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"*Third-party liability (your legal obligations to others):*\n- Compensation to customers whose data was breached\n- Defence costs in privacy or data breach litigation\n- Regulatory investigation costs and fines (where insurable)"}],["$","h2","6",{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-3","children":"The Privacy Act 2020 connection"}],["$","p","7",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"The Privacy Act 2020 created mandatory notification obligations for serious privacy breaches — meaning if your business suffers a data breach affecting customer information, you're legally required to notify the Privacy Commissioner and potentially the affected individuals."}],["$","p","8",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"Notification costs alone — letters, credit monitoring services for affected customers, legal review — can run to tens of thousands of dollars for even a modest-sized breach. Cyber insurance covers these costs."}],["$","h2","9",{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-3","children":"What a ransomware attack costs an SME"}],["$","p","10",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"Based on CERT NZ data and industry research, the average cost of a ransomware attack on an SME is $50,000-$200,000, including:\n- $10,000-$50,000 in IT recovery costs\n- $20,000-$100,000 in business interruption losses\n- $5,000-$30,000 in data recovery\n- $10,000-$50,000 in lost productivity and staff time"}],["$","p","11",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"The ransom itself (if paid) is often smaller than the recovery costs."}],["$","h2","12",{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-3","children":"What cyber insurance costs for SMEs"}],["$","p","13",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"For a typical small business with $1-5m revenue:\n- Basic cyber cover ($1m limit): $1,500-$3,000/year\n- Mid-tier cover ($2-3m limit): $3,000-$6,000/year"}],["$","p","14",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"Premiums depend on your sector (healthcare, finance, and professional services typically pay more), the nature and volume of personal data you hold, your security posture (multi-factor authentication, regular backups, and patching reduce premiums), and your turnover."}],["$","h2","15",{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-3","children":"Is it worth it?"}],["$","p","16",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"Calculate it simply: if a cyber incident would cost your business $100,000 or more in recovery and lost business, and the probability of an incident in any given year is 5-20% (which is within CERT NZ's reported range), then the expected cost is $5,000-$20,000 per year. Cyber insurance at $2,000-$4,000 per year is excellent value against that exposure."}],["$","p","17",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"The short answer for most SMEs: yes, it's worth it — especially if you hold customer personal data, rely on digital systems for operations, or process significant online payments."}],["$","h2","18",{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-3","children":"How to get cyber insurance"}],["$","p","19",{"className":"text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm my-4","children":"DUAL NZ offers cyber insurance directly through their WebRater platform for 500+ occupations. NZI, Delta Insurance, and other insurers are available through brokers including Marsh NZ. For a market comparison and tailored advice, submit a quote request through our form."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-12","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4","children":"Related Articles"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4","children":[["$","$L2","fidelity-insurance-nz-businesses",{"href":"/blog/fidelity-insurance-nz-businesses","className":"group border border-slate-2